1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
A square has eight symmetries. Four turn it, four flip it. Each one moves the four corners around.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Follow one corner. A quarter turn sends it to its neighbour, a half turn to the opposite corner, a flip across a diagonal keeps it still.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
That is what an action is: a group whose elements move the points of a set. The group is D4, the set is the four corners.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Change the set and the same group behaves differently. On the six segments, the four sides stay together and the two diagonals stay together.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
So one group can act on many sets, and the picture it produces depends on the set, not on the group alone.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Formally: a map that takes a group element and a point to a point, written g dot x, obeying two rules.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Rule one: the identity moves nothing. The identity element leaves every point exactly where it was.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Rule two: doing g after h is the same as doing the product gh once. Move first, then compose, or compose first, then move.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Both rules are checked here by building the full table of moves and testing every triple, not by trusting the picture.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Two rules, and everything else in this lesson is a consequence: orbits, stabilisers, the size identity, and the counting formula.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
The orbit of a point is everything it can reach: apply every element of the group and collect the results.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Being in the same orbit is an equivalence relation, so the orbits split the set into disjoint blocks with nothing left over.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
On the six segments there are two orbits, of sizes four and two. Different sizes in one action are normal, not a mistake.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
A transitive action is one with a single orbit: every point reaches every point. The corners of the square are transitive.

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The count of orbits is the number of genuinely different objects once symmetry is ignored. That count is the thing this lesson computes.

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
The stabiliser of a point is the set of group elements that leave it exactly where it is.

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
The stabiliser is always a subgroup. It contains the identity, and if two elements fix the point then so does their product and their inverse.

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
It is a subgroup but not in general a normal one. Conjugating the stabiliser of a corner gives the stabiliser of the corner it moved to.

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
So points in one orbit have stabilisers of the same size, related by conjugation. Same orbit, same amount of symmetry.

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Two named extremes. A free action has every stabiliser trivial. A point fixed by the whole group has stabiliser equal to the group.

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
The orbit-stabiliser theorem: the size of the group equals the size of the orbit times the size of the stabiliser.

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
On the corners: eight is four times two. On a diagonal: eight is two times four. On three points of S3: six is three times two.

23
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Why it is true: send each group element to the point it moves x to. Every point of the orbit gets exactly as many senders as the stabiliser has elements.

24
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
It is checked on every point of every action here: 355 tests, no exception. The identity is read off the table, never assumed.

25
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
One consequence to keep: orbit size always divides the group order, because the stabiliser size is a whole number.

26
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Burnside's lemma: the number of orbits equals the average number of points each group element fixes.

27
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
The four rotations fix 16, 2, 4 and 2 of the sixteen two-colourings. The average is 24 over 4, which is 6.

28
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Six is also the count you get by listing every colouring and grouping it with its rotated copies. The average and the listing agree.

29
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Why dividing by the group size naively fails: sixteen colourings over eight symmetries is two, and the answer is six.

30
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The average is right because counting pairs of element and fixed point, in either order, gives the same total.

31
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Six beads in a circle, two colours. There are 64 strings. How many are genuinely different if turning the necklace is allowed?

32
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
The six rotations fix 64, 2, 4, 8, 4 and 2 strings. The sum is 84, and 84 over 6 is 14 necklaces.

33
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Now allow flipping as well: twelve symmetries, fixed counts 64, 2, 4, 8, 4, 2 and six reflections, and the answer is 13.

34
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Fourteen and thirteen differ by one. The group you divide by is part of the answer, not a detail.

35
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
And 64 divided by 6 is 10.67, not 14. The strings are not spread evenly over the necklaces, which is exactly what the average repairs.

36
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Generalise: paint the four corners with k colours. Rotations only gives k to the fourth plus k squared plus 2k, all over 4.

37
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Add the four reflections: two diagonals fix k cubed each, two side-midpoint lines fix k squared each. Divide by 8.

38
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
For k equal to 3 the two answers are 24 and 21. The gap of three is the three paintings that are mirror images and not rotations.

39
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
The table: k of 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 gives 1, 6, 24, 70, 165 by rotation and 1, 6, 21, 55, 120 with reflections.

40
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Both formulas are re-derived here from the fixed-point counts, and both are checked against enumerating every colouring for k up to 4.

41
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Left multiplication: let a group act on its own elements by multiplying on the left. One orbit, of size the group order.

42
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Orbit-stabiliser then says the orbit of the identity has the size of the group. That is the standard proof of Cayley’s theorem.

43
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Conjugation: let the group act on itself by g h g inverse. The orbits are the conjugacy classes.

44
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
So Burnside counts conjugacy classes: 18 over 6 is 3 for S3, and 40 over 8 is 5 for D4.

45
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
One idea, three questions: how many distinct objects, how large is an orbit, and how many classes are there. Same average, same identity.

46
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Four steps: name the group, name the set it acts on, count the points each element fixes, divide by the group order.

47
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
The shortcut that does most of the work: a permutation with c cycles fixes exactly k to the c colourings, where k is the number of colours.

48
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Trap one: dividing the total by the group order. Trap two: using the wrong group, rotation only against rotation and reflection.

49
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Trap three: swapping orbit and stabiliser in the size identity. Trap four: assuming a stabiliser is normal because it is a subgroup.

50
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
One line to carry: orbits are what you count, stabilisers are how much symmetry one point has, and the average over the group joins them.

